## Porte Saint-Denis
The Porte Saint-Denis is @𝗽𝗿𝗲𝘁𝘁𝘆 much the grandest of all the surviving Paris gates, and was for many years the main entry to the city from the north. There is a triumphal arch above the gate (now bearing the Napoleonic coat of arms), and the archway through the defences is very wide. The gate was built in 1570 to a design by 𝐁𝐚𝐫𝐨𝐧 𝐝𝐞 𝐋𝐚𝐫𝐞, a French military engineer. It was one of the last major city gates to be built in Paris.

The fortifications of Paris were gradually pulled down after the Franco-Prussian war to enable the expansion of the city. Originally, the gate opened directly onto the Rue Saint-Denis, but the road was widening in stages during the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, and is now much further away. The area between the gate and the road is now a busy roundabout.

The gate itself is decorated with statues in niches. These are disposed in pairs either side of the central arch, and depict major saints of the French kingdom. From left to right, they are:
- St. Denis, the traditional founder of the French royal line;
- St. Genevieve, the patron saint of Paris;
- St. Louis, the canonised King of France;
- St. Expedite, the patron saint of lost causes and impossible tasks;
- St. Martin, the historical founder of the Merovingian dynasty;
- St. Catherine, the champion of the poor;

## Porte de Vincennes
The Porte de Vincennes was the most important of the gates on the east side of Paris. Vincennes is a town to the east of the centre of Paris, and was for centuries a separate royal domain. The gate was built in the eighteenth century, and survived largely intact through the French Revolution and the Napoleonic era.

Unusually, the gate has a single central tower rather than being symmetrical.
